  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA cut off low remains in the Great Lakes region for the next \nfew days. Moisture continues to be drawn northward into New \nEngland, while a wave of moisture passes by offshore. This \nbrings low level moisture, sprinkles, and intermittent drizzle \nthrough the first half of the day today, especially along the \nMaine coast. New Hampshire sees more of the way of breaks of sun\ntoday, especially across western areas.\n\nThe warmer conditions across western NH provide more \ninstability ahead of an approaching cold front extending from \nthe cut off low. Given the set up, a few strong to severe storms\nare possible across New Hampshire today, with wind gusts and \nhail both a concern. The marine layer looks to limit the \npotential across Maine, with a gradual weakening trend expected \nas the storms move eastward.\n\nThe front won't pass east of the area until Monday, bringing \nanother night of low clouds, fog, and scattered showers tonight \nalong the coast and in Maine. Lows generally bottom out in the \nmid 50s to low 60s across the area, with some drier air working \nin behind the front across western New Hampshire by daybreak on \nMonday.\n\nThe front will likely be passing through Maine during the day \non Monday. This brings a chance for scattered thunderstorms by \nmidday, with a chance for a strong storm possible across parts \nof Central Maine. The front then clears the area by late in the \nday, with drier air working in late in the day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nUpper-level low will continue to spin through Quebec Tuesday \nbefore finally ejecting out of the area by Wednesday. Likely \nwill have another day of diurnal clouds/brief downpours fueled \nby the cold pool aloft, focused in the higher terrain in the \nnorth. Dew points will remain comfortable with the westerly \nflow, however. On Wednesday the low loses its grip with brief \nheight rises bringing tranquil conditions. \n\nBy late week ensembles show another wave heading our way. With \na developing trough to our west, likely will see southerly flow \ndeveloping and returning humidity northward. Both the GFS and \nECMWF fully occlude this low in Canada, similar to the previous \nsystem. However the placement of the low has trended further \nnorth up towards Hudson Bay rather than central Quebec. If this \ncomes to fruition, it would suggest more of a glancing blow to \nthe trough. As such expecting forcing to be a bit more nebulous,\nbut there will still be the potential for widespread \nprecipitation if sufficient moisture can be advected into the \nregion. At minimum looks like a period of more humid conditions \nThurs-Fri with southerly flow developing. Temperatures are lower\nconfidence given cloud cover and potential onshore flow, but \nwith 850 mb temps hanging out around 13- 15 C, no signs for hot \ntemperatures. Deterministic guidance currently suggests a chance\nfor rain late Thurs especially across New Hampshire, but Friday\nwill probably bring a higher chance to the entire forecast \narea.",
